# engine.py
This file defines an ordered transaction-categorisation rule system. It represents each rule with a category, a matcher, and its source text; applies rules to transactions using first-match-wins semantics without mutating the originals; and parses human-readable rule lines into keyword- or regular-expression-based matchers, reporting malformed lines with their line numbers.

## b1, b2
Import the iterable and dataclass utilities, along with `LedgerError`, `Transaction`, and the matcher-building helpers `keyword` and `regex`. These dependencies support immutable rule records, flexible input collections, transaction copies, and the two rule-specification formats accepted by the loader.

## Rule
Represent one categorisation rule as an immutable record containing the target `category`, a callable `matcher`, and optional `source` text. Leave `source` empty by default; callers loading rules from text can retain the original cleaned line for diagnostics or later inspection.

## categorise
Accept any iterable of `Transaction` objects and an ordered list of `Rule` objects, then return a new list. For each transaction, test rules in list order and apply only the first matching rule by replacing the transaction with the result of `with_category(rule.category)`; stop checking that transaction immediately after a match. Preserve transactions unchanged when no rule matches, and rely on `with_category` so the input transaction objects are not mutated.

## load_rules
Parse each input line into a `Rule`, numbering lines from one for error messages. Strip inline comments beginning with `#` and ignore blank or comment-only lines. Require a nonempty specification after the first `:`; otherwise raise `LedgerError` with the offending line number. Interpret a specification enclosed by leading and trailing `/` characters, with more than two total characters, as a regular expression passed without its delimiters to `regex`; interpret every other specification as a comma-separated list of trimmed, nonempty keywords passed to `keyword`. Strip whitespace from the category, retain the cleaned uncommented line as the rule's `source`, preserve input order, and return the resulting list.
